66
00:02:10,358 --> 00:02:11,358
(Roar)

67
00:02:12,488 --> 00:02:13,828
(The Year of the Red Horse)

68
00:02:17,298 --> 00:02:19,968
(Rainbow Running Horses, Part 3)

69
00:02:20,998 --> 00:02:22,397
(Little Tiger Is on the Hunt, Roar!)

70
00:02:22,398 --> 00:02:23,468
"Roar."

71
00:02:25,238 --> 00:02:26,877
(Little Tiger Si Hyeon is still fast asleep in the morning.)

72
00:02:26,878 --> 00:02:27,908
Her room looks clean.

73
00:02:28,208 --> 00:02:29,447
I feel too shy to watch myself.

74
00:02:29,448 --> 00:02:30,808
(Shy)

75
00:02:37,348 --> 00:02:39,958
(She turns off the alarm right away.)

76
00:02:40,658 --> 00:02:42,788
(Getting up)

77
00:02:42,988 --> 00:02:44,927
Hello. I've been living alone for seven years.

78
00:02:44,928 --> 00:02:47,088
I'm Chef Kim Si Hyeon and my nickname is Little Tiger.

79
00:02:47,758 --> 00:02:48,758
(Roar!)

80
00:02:48,759 --> 00:02:50,198
- She's so adorable. - How cute.

81
00:02:50,398 --> 00:02:52,867
- What year were you born? - I was born in 2000.

82
00:02:52,868 --> 00:02:54,538
You've been living alone for seven years,

83
00:02:54,598 --> 00:02:56,644
so does it mean you've been on your own since high school?

84
00:02:56,668 --> 00:03:00,377
Right. I started working in 12th grade...

85
00:03:00,378 --> 00:03:02,177
- Yes. - since my winter break.

86
00:03:02,178 --> 00:03:04,047
- You started at a young age. - I've been living alone since then.

87
00:03:04,048 --> 00:03:05,847
How does someone so young get a job...

88
00:03:05,848 --> 00:03:08,717
at fine dining restaurants like three-Michelin starred restaurants?

89
00:03:08,718 --> 00:03:10,558
Did you have to take a test? How does that go?

90
00:03:10,748 --> 00:03:14,117
I got a job as soon as I graduated from high school.

91
00:03:14,118 --> 00:03:15,517
So I didn't have any work experience.

92
00:03:15,518 --> 00:03:18,527
I put together a portfolio to show all of my cooking experience.

93
00:03:18,528 --> 00:03:21,157
- Like winning an award and so on? - Yes. Something like that.

94
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:23,598
But didn't you graduate from a prestigious high school?

95
00:03:23,828 --> 00:03:26,197
Right. I graduated from Korea Culinary Arts Science High School.

96
00:03:26,198 --> 00:03:27,937
- It's a specialized high school. - Right.

97
00:03:27,938 --> 00:03:29,037
She was already on track...

98
00:03:29,038 --> 00:03:30,384
- to be an elite chef. - Yes, she was.

99
00:03:30,408 --> 00:03:31,937
But graduating from that school didn't guarantee a job...

100
00:03:31,938 --> 00:03:34,007
- at Michelin starred restaurants. - Right.

101
00:03:34,008 --> 00:03:35,937
She put together a portfolio and submitted it...

102
00:03:35,938 --> 00:03:38,248
- during interviews. - Right. I also included a letter.

103
00:03:39,278 --> 00:03:40,978
(But hold on.)

104
00:03:41,278 --> 00:03:43,347
Everyone had a lot of questions.

105
00:03:43,348 --> 00:03:45,418
She hasn't even woken up yet in the clip.

106
00:03:45,748 --> 00:03:46,917
We don't know much...

107
00:03:46,918 --> 00:03:48,358
- about the culinary world. - Right.

108
00:03:49,288 --> 00:03:51,288
(Si Hyeon finally wakes up.)

109
00:03:52,958 --> 00:03:54,597
(Current time, 7:10am)

110
00:03:54,598 --> 00:03:56,718
- She's so diligent. - Why does she wake up so early?

111
00:03:56,768 --> 00:03:57,897
I feel too shy to watch it.

112
00:03:57,898 --> 00:03:59,098
(Getting up)

113
00:03:59,938 --> 00:04:02,598
(She busily makes the bed first.)

114
00:04:04,368 --> 00:04:06,877
She's very neat. It must be because she's a chef. You're so neat.

115
00:04:06,878 --> 00:04:07,878
My gosh.

116
00:04:08,908 --> 00:04:11,147
(Turning off the light in the bedroom)

117
00:04:11,148 --> 00:04:12,808
(Turning on the light in the living room)

118
00:04:13,648 --> 00:04:14,917
(Let us introduce Si Hyeon's house.)

119
00:04:14,918 --> 00:04:16,148
My gosh. Oh, my.

120
00:04:18,088 --> 00:04:19,517
It's super clean.

121
00:04:19,518 --> 00:04:20,758
Her house looks neat and clean.

122
00:04:22,888 --> 00:04:24,228
Her pantry is in the living room.

123
00:04:26,398 --> 00:04:28,527
(Her bedroom is minimalistic.)

124
00:04:28,528 --> 00:04:30,668
It's been less than a month since I moved in.

125
00:04:31,068 --> 00:04:33,138
I don't have my own kitchen.

126
00:04:33,338 --> 00:04:34,798
So I thought it'd be nice...

127
00:04:34,968 --> 00:04:38,607
to move into a house I could also use as my studio.

128
00:04:38,608 --> 00:04:41,077
That's why I decided to move into a house with a big kitchen.

129
00:04:41,078 --> 00:04:43,078
(She settled on a house with a big kitchen.)

130
00:04:43,678 --> 00:04:47,118
(The kitchen is full of her cooking tools.)

131
00:04:47,948 --> 00:04:49,147
(Ingredients are neatly divided.)

132
00:04:49,148 --> 00:04:50,828
- How neat. - Look at how they're divided.

133
00:04:51,048 --> 00:04:52,848
This is an important part of my house.

134
00:04:53,088 --> 00:04:54,717
I need a lot of cooking tools and equipment,

135
00:04:54,718 --> 00:04:56,858
so I needed enough space to store all of them.

136
00:04:57,728 --> 00:04:59,287
(Dishware fills up the massive pantry.)

137
00:04:59,288 --> 00:05:00,757
- You have so many pretty dishes. - Dishes.

138
00:05:00,758 --> 00:05:02,197
You have a pantry...

139
00:05:02,198 --> 00:05:03,758
- where most people have a TV. - Right.

140
00:05:05,998 --> 00:05:07,498
I also have a lot of appliances.

141
00:05:07,738 --> 00:05:09,538
I have a vacuum sealer,

142
00:05:10,438 --> 00:05:12,938
an ice cream machine, a food dehydrator,

143
00:05:13,878 --> 00:05:16,178
a blender, and a sous vide machine.

144
00:05:18,108 --> 00:05:19,108
A pasta maker.

145
00:05:19,978 --> 00:05:21,748
- You have all of these. - I have that.

146
00:05:22,118 --> 00:05:24,548
I've been buying them over these seven years.

147
00:05:24,818 --> 00:05:26,488
So I ended up with a lot of equipment.

148
00:05:26,988 --> 00:05:30,258
The whole rack is like my treasure box.

149
00:05:31,628 --> 00:05:33,757
(She walks past her treasure box...)

150
00:05:33,758 --> 00:05:36,268
(and goes to the bathroom.)

151
00:05:36,898 --> 00:05:39,198
- Oh, no! - Why?

152
00:05:39,898 --> 00:05:41,168
- You look the same. - Well...

153
00:05:41,298 --> 00:05:42,867
- Considering everything, - Yes.

154
00:05:42,868 --> 00:05:44,107
- you look great. - Really?

155
00:05:44,108 --> 00:05:45,337
There isn't a single embarrassing moment.

156
00:05:45,338 --> 00:05:47,138
You should watch one of my episodes.

157
00:05:47,678 --> 00:05:48,807
Oh, my.

158
00:05:48,808 --> 00:05:50,408
(You'll feel very relieved.)

159
00:05:51,078 --> 00:05:53,248
(Checking her perfectly-fine appearance)

160
00:05:54,478 --> 00:05:56,388
(Let's see.)

161
00:05:57,048 --> 00:05:59,188
(Rattling)

162
00:06:02,758 --> 00:06:04,257
(She starts cutting her nails.)

163
00:06:04,258 --> 00:06:05,818
She's cutting her nails in the morning.

164
00:06:07,998 --> 00:06:10,628
It's my habit to check my nails before washing my face.

165
00:06:10,928 --> 00:06:12,268
When you have long fingernails,

166
00:06:12,368 --> 00:06:14,408
you sometimes end up chopping off your fingernails.

167
00:06:14,698 --> 00:06:17,208
Or your fingernails could end up in food.

168
00:06:17,408 --> 00:06:20,977
I ended up fixating on having short fingernails.

169
00:06:20,978 --> 00:06:22,578
"My fingernails must be short."

170
00:06:23,578 --> 00:06:25,807
(She cuts her fingernails before washing her face as usual.)

171
00:06:25,808 --> 00:06:29,318
(Short)

172
00:06:30,048 --> 00:06:32,817
(Then she brushes her teeth...)

173
00:06:32,818 --> 00:06:34,658
(and washes her face.)

174
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:38,288
(Refreshed)

175
00:06:38,928 --> 00:06:41,028
(Giggling)

176
00:06:41,758 --> 00:06:44,998
(She quietly sits next to Wilson.)

177
00:06:45,768 --> 00:06:46,898
This is crazy.

178
00:06:49,238 --> 00:06:54,378
(Let me take a picture with him.)

179
00:06:55,408 --> 00:06:57,177
(Making a V)

180
00:06:57,178 --> 00:06:58,947
- I couldn't believe it. - You look like a kid.

181
00:06:58,948 --> 00:06:59,948
She looks so young.

182
00:07:00,748 --> 00:07:03,348
(Smiling)

183
00:07:03,948 --> 00:07:06,318
(After taking a picture with Wilson, )

184
00:07:07,558 --> 00:07:10,188
(she goes straight to the fridge.)

185
00:07:11,128 --> 00:07:12,257
I'm curious...

186
00:07:12,258 --> 00:07:14,378
- about what she has in her fridge. - Right, exactly.

187
00:07:14,728 --> 00:07:15,997
(Her fridge is neatly organized.)

188
00:07:15,998 --> 00:07:17,504
- Gosh, her fridge too? - That's her fridge.

189
00:07:17,528 --> 00:07:18,928
It's well-organized.

190
00:07:19,598 --> 00:07:21,567
She labeled everything in the fridge.

191
00:07:21,568 --> 00:07:23,838
(She thoroughly put on labels.)

192
00:07:24,468 --> 00:07:25,507
(There's also food she pickled herself.)

193
00:07:25,508 --> 00:07:27,837
- You also have long-lasting food. - She wrote down the dates.

194
00:07:27,838 --> 00:07:29,847
- She included dates in the labels. - I see. So...

195
00:07:29,848 --> 00:07:32,778
I have to let them age for a specific number of days.

196
00:07:33,848 --> 00:07:36,318
Personally, I find food...

197
00:07:36,448 --> 00:07:39,417
like marmalades, pickles, and kimchi...

198
00:07:39,418 --> 00:07:42,118
that require fermentation and aging processes fascinating.

199
00:07:42,218 --> 00:07:45,287
So I like storing food before I consume it.

200
00:07:45,288 --> 00:07:47,004
(She's aging seasonal food all over her house.)

201
00:07:47,028 --> 00:07:48,958
(Opening up the vegetable basket)

202
00:07:50,128 --> 00:07:51,167
(I'll go with you, shepherd's purse.)

203
00:07:51,168 --> 00:07:53,688
- Shepherd's purse is in season. - Shepherd's purse is amazing.

204
00:07:55,168 --> 00:07:57,468
(She turns on the light in the kitchen first.)

205
00:07:58,138 --> 00:08:01,108
(She starts cooking in earnest.)

206
00:08:01,678 --> 00:08:03,378
I'm so curious about her breakfast.

207
00:08:06,308 --> 00:08:08,818
(She puts a pot of water on the stove.)

208
00:08:09,448 --> 00:08:12,318
(She prepares all kinds of seasoning like chili powder and doenjang.)

209
00:08:12,718 --> 00:08:15,388
(Let's start cooking.)

210
00:08:16,088 --> 00:08:17,757
I think her gaze changed a bit.

211
00:08:17,758 --> 00:08:19,758
(Putting on a fierce gaze like Little Tiger)

212
00:08:21,198 --> 00:08:22,758
- She's tying up her hair. - Like that?

213
00:08:22,928 --> 00:08:24,098
That means business.

214
00:08:24,568 --> 00:08:27,868
(It's time for Little Tiger to reveal her claws.)

215
00:08:28,668 --> 00:08:30,668
(Aiming at her target)

216
00:08:31,708 --> 00:08:33,038
(Washing)

217
00:08:33,378 --> 00:08:34,937
(Wash the shepherd's purse in cold water.)

218
00:08:34,938 --> 00:08:36,298
Gosh, shepherd's purse looks good.

219
00:08:38,008 --> 00:08:40,148
- It's great in doenjang soup. - Right.

220
00:08:40,448 --> 00:08:41,578
I love shepherd's purse.

221
00:08:43,848 --> 00:08:46,588
(Her hunt on shepherd's purse is going smoothly.)

222
00:08:47,618 --> 00:08:49,388
(Rustling)

223
00:08:50,388 --> 00:08:51,587
(She brings out a bag of ramyeon.)

224
00:08:51,588 --> 00:08:53,428
- What? - What?

225
00:08:54,128 --> 00:08:55,928
I love ramyeon.

226
00:08:55,998 --> 00:08:57,197
(Giggling)

227
00:08:57,198 --> 00:08:59,468
I tried cooking with different brands of ramyeon.

228
00:08:59,798 --> 00:09:02,268
I also tried different recipes to cook ramyeon.

229
00:09:02,568 --> 00:09:06,738
I had some leftover shepherd's purse from yesterday.

230
00:09:07,108 --> 00:09:09,377
So I made doenjang ramyeon with shepherd's purse.

231
00:09:09,378 --> 00:09:11,608
My gosh. You ate ramyeon for breakfast?

232
00:09:11,678 --> 00:09:12,947
I thought you'd make stew.

233
00:09:12,948 --> 00:09:14,947
- Right. Like doenjang ramyeon. - Exactly.

234
00:09:14,948 --> 00:09:17,517
I'm curious about the pairing of ramyeon and shepherd's purse.

235
00:09:17,518 --> 00:09:18,688
Shepherd's purse ramyeon?

236
00:09:20,058 --> 00:09:22,757
It's just like cooking ramyeon. I added the seasoning packet.

237
00:09:22,758 --> 00:09:24,518
(She starts by adding the seasoning packet.)

238
00:09:24,828 --> 00:09:27,128
Then I added a tablespoon of doenjang of my choice.

239
00:09:27,558 --> 00:09:29,498
You're adding doenjang to the ramyeon.

240
00:09:31,328 --> 00:09:32,997
(Then she adds the noodles to the doenjang broth.)

241
00:09:32,998 --> 00:09:35,538
- I love that brand of ramyeon. - Right? That's tasty.

242
00:09:35,938 --> 00:09:38,667
If I'm craving something spicy,

243
00:09:38,668 --> 00:09:41,238
- I'd use the spicy ramyeon. - The spicy ramyeon.

244
00:09:41,438 --> 00:09:43,378
But since I was having it for breakfast,

245
00:09:43,878 --> 00:09:47,778
I wanted to be able to taste shepherd's purse and doenjang.

246
00:09:47,918 --> 00:09:50,087
So I went with the potato noodles.

247
00:09:50,088 --> 00:09:51,794
(She chose the ramyeon with potato noodles.)

248
00:09:51,818 --> 00:09:53,458
(Adding chili powder)

249
00:09:54,258 --> 00:09:56,058
When the noodles are about cooked,

250
00:09:56,258 --> 00:09:57,688
add chopped shepherd's purse.

251
00:09:58,128 --> 00:09:59,227
It's a restaurant dish.

252
00:09:59,228 --> 00:10:00,228
(Grabbing)

253
00:10:00,398 --> 00:10:02,357
I need to add a bit of the fish sauce at the end.

254
00:10:02,358 --> 00:10:03,467
(The last ingredient is a bit of the anchovy fish sauce.)

255
00:10:03,468 --> 00:10:04,527
(Fish sauce?)

256
00:10:04,528 --> 00:10:05,848
You're adding anchovy fish sauce?

257
00:10:06,328 --> 00:10:07,598
Anchovy fish sauce?

258
00:10:07,938 --> 00:10:09,038
That looks tasty.

259
00:10:09,768 --> 00:10:12,608
(It's all ready.)

260
00:10:13,678 --> 00:10:15,878
- It was really good. - That does look good.

261
00:10:16,108 --> 00:10:18,577
- Many people will try this recipe. - Totally.

262
00:10:18,578 --> 00:10:20,594
(Her doenjang ramyeon with shepherd's purse is ready.)

263
00:10:20,618 --> 00:10:23,788
(Pulling)

264
00:10:24,648 --> 00:10:26,858
(Steaming)

265
00:10:29,288 --> 00:10:31,287
(Taking a big bite)

266
00:10:31,288 --> 00:10:33,608
- She's eating with gusto. - Gosh, that sounds appetizing.

267
00:10:33,758 --> 00:10:35,698
(Satisfied)

268
00:10:37,728 --> 00:10:40,568
(She only eats the shepherd's purse this time.)

269
00:10:41,898 --> 00:10:44,438
(Oh, right.)

270
00:10:45,338 --> 00:10:46,707
(She watches a dating show during her meal.)

271
00:10:46,708 --> 00:10:47,777
A dating show.

272
00:10:47,778 --> 00:10:49,538
(She watches a dating show during her meal.)

273
00:10:50,948 --> 00:10:53,208
(Smiling)

274
00:10:55,778 --> 00:10:56,818
This is crazy.

275
00:10:58,648 --> 00:11:01,218
(While her eyes are fixed on her phone, )

276
00:11:01,988 --> 00:11:05,028
(she inhales the noodles nonstop.)

277
00:11:07,198 --> 00:11:10,298
(She picks up a chopstickful of shepherd's purse.)

278
00:11:12,128 --> 00:11:14,067
(The fragrant aroma spreads in her mouth.)

279
00:11:14,068 --> 00:11:16,137
How does shepherd's purse taste?

280
00:11:16,138 --> 00:11:18,308
- It's slightly bitter. - Bitter?

281
00:11:18,408 --> 00:11:21,208
Here's the thing. It's cold right now.

282
00:11:21,308 --> 00:11:23,038
As for shepherd's purse from winter,

283
00:11:23,178 --> 00:11:24,947
it holds onto a lot of sugar...

284
00:11:24,948 --> 00:11:26,678
- not to freeze underground. - Right.

285
00:11:27,018 --> 00:11:30,418
- So it's sweeter when it's cooked. - I see.

286
00:11:30,618 --> 00:11:32,564
- It's actually sweet. - It's packed with nutrients.

287
00:11:32,588 --> 00:11:36,118
Underground winter vegetables like spring cabbages,

288
00:11:36,388 --> 00:11:37,734
shepherd's purse, spinach, and winter spinach...

289
00:11:37,758 --> 00:11:39,027
- are all tasty. - They're all sweet.

290
00:11:39,028 --> 00:11:40,297
- Right. - No way.

291
00:11:40,298 --> 00:11:41,597
- They hold onto sugars underground. - Then...

292
00:11:41,598 --> 00:11:43,327
After hearing your explanation,

293
00:11:43,328 --> 00:11:45,867
I'd love to have some vegetables even when I'm not a fan of them.

294
00:11:45,868 --> 00:11:46,897
- Really? - Yes.

295
00:11:46,898 --> 00:11:47,937
- I should buy it right away. - I'll go and buy some.

296
00:11:47,938 --> 00:11:49,978
- I'll buy it right away. - I should go and buy it.

297
00:11:50,638 --> 00:11:53,638
(She drinks it up from the pot while smelling the aroma.)

298
00:11:55,108 --> 00:11:57,848
(That was a good meal.)

299
00:11:58,548 --> 00:12:00,277
(Empty)

300
00:12:00,278 --> 00:12:02,378
- She eats a lot. - She really does.

301
00:12:02,678 --> 00:12:05,718
(She lets down her hair since she's done with her hunt.)

302
00:12:06,718 --> 00:12:08,318
(Splashing)

303
00:12:09,188 --> 00:12:10,757
(She washes the dishes as soon as she's done eating.)

304
00:12:10,758 --> 00:12:12,458
I love that you're so clean and neat.

305
00:12:14,398 --> 00:12:16,868
(Tying her hair)

306
00:12:17,598 --> 00:12:20,068
(She ties up her hair again.)

307
00:12:21,038 --> 00:12:23,638
(It hasn't even been 8am yet.)

308
00:12:24,508 --> 00:12:27,908
(Lifting)

309
00:12:29,538 --> 00:12:30,977
- Is that a stone cutting board? - She's taking out a cutting board.

310
00:12:30,978 --> 00:12:33,394
- It looks like she'll cook again. - She looks so professional.

311
00:12:33,418 --> 00:12:34,547
You're very different...

312
00:12:34,548 --> 00:12:35,718
- with your hair up. - Right.

313
00:12:36,248 --> 00:12:37,288
My chef side is on now.

314
00:12:39,918 --> 00:12:41,058
- What? - Hallabong?

315
00:12:41,288 --> 00:12:42,448
What are those? They're huge.

316
00:12:42,518 --> 00:12:44,087
- They're so big. - They look like hallabong.

317
00:12:44,088 --> 00:12:45,358
- They're hallabong. - Really?

318
00:12:46,388 --> 00:12:49,158
(She had bought a bunch of massive-sized fruit.)

319
00:12:49,498 --> 00:12:51,498
(Her sink is full now.)

320
00:12:51,728 --> 00:12:53,538
You can only have them now.

321
00:12:54,268 --> 00:12:56,438
They're called daeng yuzu.

322
00:12:56,598 --> 00:12:58,198
- It's a native variety of yuzu. - Yuzu.

323
00:12:58,308 --> 00:12:59,567
It's grown here.

324
00:12:59,568 --> 00:13:01,678
- That's as big as my face. - Seriously.

325
00:13:01,878 --> 00:13:03,158
- You can even roll it. - Right.

326
00:13:03,378 --> 00:13:04,548
It's huge.

327
00:13:05,878 --> 00:13:07,947
- I've never seen daeng yuzu before. - It's so big.

328
00:13:07,948 --> 00:13:08,948
(Fascinated)

329
00:13:10,118 --> 00:13:12,988
(She also cleans the bowl.)

330
00:13:14,488 --> 00:13:16,458
(Spraying)

331
00:13:16,828 --> 00:13:22,628
(She rubs alcohol disinfectant on the bowl.)

332
00:13:25,098 --> 00:13:26,098
(Grabbing)

333
00:13:29,368 --> 00:13:34,008
(Chopping)

334
00:13:36,948 --> 00:13:40,878
(Skillfully preparing daeng yuzu)

335
00:13:42,048 --> 00:13:43,988
(Focused)

336
00:13:45,188 --> 00:13:46,688
The peels are thick.

337
00:13:46,858 --> 00:13:48,318
- That looks good. - Right.

338
00:13:49,158 --> 00:13:52,388
(Separating fruit flesh and peels without hesitation)

339
00:13:54,428 --> 00:13:56,827
I'm going to make daeng yuzu marmalade today.

340
00:13:56,828 --> 00:13:57,928
She's making a marmalade.

341
00:13:58,198 --> 00:14:00,798
As it's an unfamiliar marmalade to everyone,

342
00:14:01,098 --> 00:14:02,938
daeng yuzu is hard to come by.

343
00:14:03,138 --> 00:14:05,968
The first time I tasted it, I thought it tasted amazing.

344
00:14:06,708 --> 00:14:08,407
I was so sad that I didn't have enough...

345
00:14:08,408 --> 00:14:10,077
to make marmalades for next year.

346
00:14:10,078 --> 00:14:13,818
I didn't want the next-year me to feel sad.

347
00:14:14,148 --> 00:14:15,618
So I've been making it every year.

348
00:14:17,348 --> 00:14:18,417
It's your secret weapon.

349
00:14:18,418 --> 00:14:20,218
(Chopping them nonstop to make the marmalade)

350
00:14:21,058 --> 00:14:22,958
- You're left-handed. - Yes, I am.

351
00:14:23,088 --> 00:14:25,028
Lefties look cool for some reason.

352
00:14:29,228 --> 00:14:32,098
(She cleans the cutting board right after using it.)

353
00:14:33,228 --> 00:14:35,738
(She grabs her knife again right after separating the flesh.)

354
00:14:40,138 --> 00:14:41,407
Wait, do people eat that part too?

355
00:14:41,408 --> 00:14:43,178
(What's that?)

356
00:14:45,308 --> 00:14:49,218
(She carves out a thin strip of the peel.)

357
00:14:53,048 --> 00:14:56,518
(Delicate)

358
00:14:58,128 --> 00:15:01,658
It's crazy that she can make it as thin as that.

359
00:15:02,058 --> 00:15:04,197
The white part is a bit bitter.

360
00:15:04,198 --> 00:15:06,838
- There's bitterness to it. Yes. - Just like tangerines.

361
00:15:06,938 --> 00:15:07,938
- Like tangerines. - Yes.

362
00:15:07,939 --> 00:15:09,297
So I remove the white part...

363
00:15:09,298 --> 00:15:10,984
- and leave only the zest on. - That must be...

364
00:15:11,008 --> 00:15:12,608
- So you're using the zest. - Yes.

365
00:15:13,068 --> 00:15:15,838
That's so fascinating. Look at her slicing that out.

366
00:15:17,378 --> 00:15:20,108
(The fruit is getting separated into three different layers.)

367
00:15:20,818 --> 00:15:23,278
(Feeling tired)

368
00:15:23,888 --> 00:15:25,087
(Her wrist aches from constant slicing.)

369
00:15:25,088 --> 00:15:26,418
Her wrist must hurt.

370
00:15:27,388 --> 00:15:29,558
(Stopping)

371
00:15:29,988 --> 00:15:33,088
(She suddenly stops cutting.)

372
00:15:35,358 --> 00:15:36,358
(She washes her hand and comes back.)

373
00:15:36,359 --> 00:15:37,839
The entire process is clean and neat.

374
00:15:38,268 --> 00:15:40,188
- Even the alignment of the zest. - I love that.

375
00:15:40,668 --> 00:15:42,098
(She makes coffee.)

376
00:15:43,938 --> 00:15:45,638
(Slicing the zest)

377
00:15:46,368 --> 00:15:48,138
(Twisting)

378
00:15:52,048 --> 00:15:54,548
That's yuzu coffee.

379
00:15:55,448 --> 00:15:59,388
(She coats the rim of her coffee cup with the daeng yuzu zest.)

380
00:16:00,258 --> 00:16:01,517
(This hits the spot.)

381
00:16:01,518 --> 00:16:03,058
You might've seen it at cocktail bars.

382
00:16:03,388 --> 00:16:06,127
The part of the glass where your lips touch...

383
00:16:06,128 --> 00:16:07,698
is called the rim.

384
00:16:07,928 --> 00:16:11,168
When you twist a thin slice of the zest...

385
00:16:11,698 --> 00:16:12,927
on the rim,

386
00:16:12,928 --> 00:16:15,797
the oily layer of the fruit pops...

387
00:16:15,798 --> 00:16:17,198
and gives a nice citrusy fragrance.

388
00:16:17,668 --> 00:16:20,577
So since I was prepping daeng yuzu, I thought about trying that out.

389
00:16:20,578 --> 00:16:22,478
So I did. And it smelled so fresh.

390
00:16:23,408 --> 00:16:25,748
(She resumes after recharging on caffeine.)

391
00:16:26,148 --> 00:16:27,678
(Focused)

392
00:16:30,048 --> 00:16:32,547
(Julienning the yuzu with swift wrist movements)

393
00:16:32,548 --> 00:16:33,817
- That's the amazing knife skill... - Right.

394
00:16:33,818 --> 00:16:35,888
Chef Kim Hee Eun approved of.

395
00:16:36,388 --> 00:16:37,458
"Amazing."

396
00:16:37,758 --> 00:16:39,158
That's really hard.

397
00:16:40,528 --> 00:16:42,898
(She keeps on chopping.)

398
00:16:43,968 --> 00:16:46,198
(Making a pile of yuzu strips in consistent thickness)

399
00:16:46,828 --> 00:16:49,698
(Her hand movements are machine-like.)

400
00:16:49,868 --> 00:16:51,808
(Impressed)

401
00:16:53,608 --> 00:16:54,678
All done.

402
00:16:54,778 --> 00:16:56,007
She cut everything?

403
00:16:56,008 --> 00:16:57,478
They look like chips.

404
00:16:57,808 --> 00:16:58,878
They do.

405
00:16:59,908 --> 00:17:03,178
(It took 1 hour and 30 minutes to prepare the ingredient.)

406
00:17:03,948 --> 00:17:06,148
(Whirring)

407
00:17:06,818 --> 00:17:09,387
(Pouring the blended flesh over a strainer)

408
00:17:09,388 --> 00:17:10,987
Without that process,

409
00:17:10,988 --> 00:17:12,658
the marmalade gets too pulpy.

410
00:17:12,858 --> 00:17:14,997
- So I run it through a strainer. - She'll use that for the marmalade.

411
00:17:14,998 --> 00:17:16,058
- I see. - Yes.

412
00:17:16,598 --> 00:17:18,528
(She combines the zest and juice.)

413
00:17:19,898 --> 00:17:20,937
It's sugar.

414
00:17:20,938 --> 00:17:22,798
(Adding sugar in an equal amount)

415
00:17:23,808 --> 00:17:25,138
(Stirring)

416
00:17:26,608 --> 00:17:28,208
- That looks great. - Look at the color.

417
00:17:28,238 --> 00:17:29,538
How do you usually eat that?

418
00:17:29,678 --> 00:17:30,978
Do you make tea with it?

419
00:17:31,048 --> 00:17:32,307
You can make tea with it.

420
00:17:32,308 --> 00:17:34,017
Or you can add it to sparkling water.

421
00:17:34,018 --> 00:17:36,058
- Sparkling water? - Yes, that's one way to eat it.

422
00:17:36,148 --> 00:17:38,518
- You can add it to peanut porridge. - Yes.

423
00:17:38,648 --> 00:17:40,517
Sometimes, I use it instead of plum syrup.

424
00:17:40,518 --> 00:17:41,687
You can replace sugar...

425
00:17:41,688 --> 00:17:42,918
- with it. - Right.

426
00:17:43,558 --> 00:17:45,758
(All she has to do now is wait for it to age.)

427
00:17:46,458 --> 00:17:47,927
(Be tasty.)

428
00:17:47,928 --> 00:17:49,697
- You have to let it age for a year? - Right.

429
00:17:49,698 --> 00:17:51,627
- I let it age for over a year. - My gosh.

430
00:17:51,628 --> 00:17:53,867
I now see that giving marmalade as a present...

431
00:17:53,868 --> 00:17:55,938
- was something truly special. - Of course.

432
00:17:56,098 --> 00:17:57,898
- It requires a lot of attention. - I also...

433
00:17:59,768 --> 00:18:01,107
You must feel proud that you made it.

434
00:18:01,108 --> 00:18:03,008
Right. I felt great.

435
00:18:04,308 --> 00:18:06,908
(Her kitchen is spotless too.)

436
00:18:09,048 --> 00:18:11,318
(She goes to the bathroom after finishing a long task.)

437
00:18:11,848 --> 00:18:13,447
(Touching)

438
00:18:13,448 --> 00:18:14,757
(A sharp inhale)

439
00:18:14,758 --> 00:18:16,118
I don't need to wash my hair.

440
00:18:17,188 --> 00:18:18,628
Oh, no.

441
00:18:19,088 --> 00:18:20,688
It's okay. I don't wash up often either.

442
00:18:22,198 --> 00:18:23,927
It's a hassle to wash hair when it's long.

443
00:18:23,928 --> 00:18:25,598
I usually wash my hair at night...

444
00:18:25,898 --> 00:18:27,237
because of the oil from food.

445
00:18:27,238 --> 00:18:28,267
- So I wash it at night. - Right.

446
00:18:28,268 --> 00:18:30,108
Then I check how my hair looks in the morning.

447
00:18:31,308 --> 00:18:33,208
(Thoroughly dabbing on her face)

448
00:18:33,778 --> 00:18:35,177
(Wearing her hair down)

449
00:18:35,178 --> 00:18:36,338
She's letting her hair down.

450
00:18:37,748 --> 00:18:39,147
(Putting on a jacket)

451
00:18:39,148 --> 00:18:40,248
She's going out.

452
00:18:40,548 --> 00:18:43,118
(She gets ready to go out in an instant.)

453
00:18:43,518 --> 00:18:45,188
(Turning off the light)

454
00:18:47,618 --> 00:18:49,718
(Opening up)

455
00:18:51,228 --> 00:18:53,127
(She takes out a cart from the shoe closet.)

456
00:18:53,128 --> 00:18:55,028
That must be her grocery cart.

457
00:18:55,658 --> 00:18:57,627
- Don't moms always carry one? - Right.

458
00:18:57,628 --> 00:18:58,628
(There's one more?)

459
00:18:59,698 --> 00:19:00,798
This is too hard.

460
00:19:02,868 --> 00:19:06,138
(The bag that matches today's outfit is...)

461
00:19:07,378 --> 00:19:09,337
What's the dilemma? Your fashion?

462
00:19:09,338 --> 00:19:10,748
(Finding her adorable)

463
00:19:12,778 --> 00:19:13,947
(The cute dotted cart is chosen.)

464
00:19:13,948 --> 00:19:15,648
I guess she factors that into her outfit.

465
00:19:15,678 --> 00:19:17,024
She must try to match it to her outfit.

466
00:19:17,048 --> 00:19:18,587
"Which one should I pick..."

467
00:19:18,588 --> 00:19:19,888
"for today's look?"

468
00:19:20,188 --> 00:19:22,548
I've never seen anyone think that long about grocery carts.

469
00:19:25,688 --> 00:19:27,297
(Si Hyeon comes out with the dotted grocery cart.)

470
00:19:27,298 --> 00:19:28,627
- How cute. - It looks like...

471
00:19:28,628 --> 00:19:30,348
- her mom sent her out on an errand. - Yes.

472
00:19:30,528 --> 00:19:31,668
"Buy a block of tofu."

473
00:19:32,398 --> 00:19:33,428
"Okay."

474
00:19:34,868 --> 00:19:38,208
(Running to finish her errand)

475
00:19:40,908 --> 00:19:43,237
(She runs until she's out of breath.)

476
00:19:43,238 --> 00:19:44,577
Where's she going?

477
00:19:44,578 --> 00:19:45,748
(Where is she going?)

478
00:19:48,148 --> 00:19:49,148
The bus stop.

479
00:19:49,149 --> 00:19:50,669
(She sits on the bench at a bus stop.)

480
00:19:53,248 --> 00:19:54,888
(Spotting)

481
00:19:56,518 --> 00:19:59,528
(The bus arrives just in time.)

482
00:20:00,928 --> 00:20:02,297
Do you not drive?

483
00:20:02,298 --> 00:20:03,998
I don't have a driver's license.

484
00:20:04,928 --> 00:20:06,768
- I take the bus. - Taking the bus is...

485
00:20:07,068 --> 00:20:08,167
the most convenient in Seoul.

486
00:20:08,168 --> 00:20:09,298
Right.

487
00:20:10,338 --> 00:20:12,937
(She freely heads to her destination on a bus.)

488
00:20:12,938 --> 00:20:14,338
That's the perk of taking the bus.

489
00:20:15,678 --> 00:20:18,278
I guess there's a specific place you want to go to.

490
00:20:18,678 --> 00:20:20,518
Right. I go there often.

491
00:20:21,678 --> 00:20:24,048
(Getting up)

492
00:20:27,558 --> 00:20:31,828
(Si Hyeon gets off at her regular spot.)

493
00:20:35,198 --> 00:20:37,828
I've arrived at Gyeongdong Market.

494
00:20:37,998 --> 00:20:40,068
You went to Gyeongdong Market.

495
00:20:41,138 --> 00:20:44,908
(It stretches out right next to the bus stop.)

496
00:20:45,508 --> 00:20:49,578
I've been coming to Gyeongdong Market for a very long time.

497
00:20:50,978 --> 00:20:52,878
- I think it's the biggest market. - It is.

498
00:20:53,008 --> 00:20:54,117
One wrong turn, and you get lost.

499
00:20:54,118 --> 00:20:55,218
- It's that big. - Right.

500
00:20:56,018 --> 00:20:57,787
(Si Hyeon's hunting ground is filled with ingredients.)

501
00:20:57,788 --> 00:20:59,048
"Hunting ground."

502
00:20:59,918 --> 00:21:04,128
The market always has fresh ingredients.

503
00:21:04,488 --> 00:21:05,828
So I go there more often.

504
00:21:07,528 --> 00:21:09,858
(Si Hyeon is roaming the market to look for her prey.)

505
00:21:10,528 --> 00:21:14,198
(Smoothly squeezing through the crowd)

506
00:21:16,138 --> 00:21:17,868
It's so crowded today.

507
00:21:20,208 --> 00:21:22,808
(She stops in front of a crowded stall.)

508
00:21:23,178 --> 00:21:24,708
- Hello. - Here you go.

509
00:21:26,378 --> 00:21:29,647
(It's impossible to skip on twisted donuts at markets.)

510
00:21:29,648 --> 00:21:30,747
They're so good.

511
00:21:30,748 --> 00:21:32,147
The twisted donuts are the best at markets.

512
00:21:32,148 --> 00:21:33,588
- My gosh. - Goodness.

513
00:21:33,688 --> 00:21:34,917
I didn't want my sugar to crash.

514
00:21:34,918 --> 00:21:37,927
So I went to the twisted donut stall first.

515
00:21:37,928 --> 00:21:39,528
She knows what's up.

516
00:21:41,428 --> 00:21:43,597
- Eating it fresh is important. - Right.

517
00:21:43,598 --> 00:21:45,427
- You must eat it right away. - Here you go.

518
00:21:45,428 --> 00:21:47,368
Thank you. Have a good day.

519
00:21:50,968 --> 00:21:52,237
You know your stuff.

520
00:21:52,238 --> 00:21:53,868
- You kept the bag open. - Of course.

521
00:21:54,408 --> 00:21:56,248
If you close up the bag, the donuts get soggy.

522
00:21:56,538 --> 00:21:58,077
I did that once.

523
00:21:58,078 --> 00:21:59,877
And my friend criticized me for it big time.

524
00:21:59,878 --> 00:22:00,878
- I'd defriend you right away. - Hey.

525
00:22:00,879 --> 00:22:02,547
- You really shouldn't do that. - I should cut ties with you.

526
00:22:02,548 --> 00:22:04,717
- The cold air was going into it. - Gosh. What are you talking about?

527
00:22:04,718 --> 00:22:06,447
If you close up the bag...

528
00:22:06,448 --> 00:22:07,658
- Dear me. - Gosh.

529
00:22:07,718 --> 00:22:08,718
I got scolded again.

530
00:22:10,318 --> 00:22:11,988
(Taking a bite before it gets soggy)

531
00:22:12,628 --> 00:22:13,988
- Gosh, that looks good. - Oh, my.

532
00:22:14,228 --> 00:22:16,058
(Humming)

533
00:22:17,328 --> 00:22:19,628
(A big bite of the twisted donut...)

534
00:22:20,638 --> 00:22:21,897
(puts her in a good mood.)

535
00:22:21,898 --> 00:22:23,138
She looks happy.

536
00:22:23,768 --> 00:22:25,708
(Taking a bite even at the chaotic market)

537
00:22:26,308 --> 00:22:28,408
(Wiping her fingers on her pants)

538
00:22:28,908 --> 00:22:30,238
(Glancing)

539
00:22:31,548 --> 00:22:34,017
(She starts walking around the market in earnest.)

540
00:22:34,018 --> 00:22:35,377
- Hello. - I'm curious about...

541
00:22:35,378 --> 00:22:36,418
what you'll buy there.

542
00:22:36,718 --> 00:22:38,718
- Hello. - Hello.

543
00:22:39,388 --> 00:22:41,518
- They look great. - Here.

544
00:22:42,918 --> 00:22:45,258
(A stall with a lot of greenery catches her eye.)

545
00:22:45,358 --> 00:22:46,988
I go to this vegetable stall regularly.

546
00:22:47,228 --> 00:22:49,098
They look great. Oh, my.

547
00:22:50,868 --> 00:22:52,997
There are so many ingredients you can buy...

548
00:22:52,998 --> 00:22:54,167
now that I'm looking at them at once.

549
00:22:54,168 --> 00:22:55,498
And the prices are so reasonable.

550
00:22:55,598 --> 00:22:58,038
Overseas, people cook with herbs.

551
00:22:58,138 --> 00:23:00,708
In Korea, we have our greens.

552
00:23:00,838 --> 00:23:04,178
Their fragrances are all different.

553
00:23:04,378 --> 00:23:06,407
And we have to handle them differently too.

554
00:23:06,408 --> 00:23:09,578
To me, this is like a department store.

555
00:23:10,648 --> 00:23:12,247
I love greens.

556
00:23:12,248 --> 00:23:13,288
I watched your episode.

557
00:23:14,458 --> 00:23:15,558
Hello.

558
00:23:15,758 --> 00:23:17,487
I bought some for you when I bought mine.

559
00:23:17,488 --> 00:23:19,988
- I got you twisted donuts. - That was good of you.

560
00:23:20,088 --> 00:23:22,357
- They're good. - Aren't you guys...

561
00:23:22,358 --> 00:23:23,428
- already close? - We are.

562
00:23:25,068 --> 00:23:27,128
(Let me hunt down some greens.)

563
00:23:27,268 --> 00:23:29,398
- Two bundles of spring cabbages. - Okay.

564
00:23:29,638 --> 00:23:31,208
I'll take those today.

565
00:23:31,838 --> 00:23:33,107
(Picking the spring cabbages without hesitation)

566
00:23:33,108 --> 00:23:34,608
Spring cabbages are so good.

567
00:23:35,478 --> 00:23:36,708
They look so tasty.

568
00:23:37,778 --> 00:23:39,707
I'll take the island spinach. Not the cleaned ones.

569
00:23:39,708 --> 00:23:41,117
- Sure. The ones with dirt. - Island spinach?

570
00:23:41,118 --> 00:23:42,778
It's a type of spinach.

571
00:23:43,148 --> 00:23:45,394
- Give me ones with a lot of roots. - This one is thick. Sure.

572
00:23:45,418 --> 00:23:46,647
How much do you want?

573
00:23:46,648 --> 00:23:48,688
I just need about 375g of it.

574
00:23:49,388 --> 00:23:50,517
- This? - This looks good.

575
00:23:50,518 --> 00:23:51,558
- Do you want it? - Yes.

576
00:23:51,888 --> 00:23:53,857
The roots are tiny. Are you okay with that?

577
00:23:53,858 --> 00:23:55,028
- That looks great. - Sure.

578
00:23:55,658 --> 00:23:58,898
Island spinach is really tasty right now.

579
00:23:59,168 --> 00:24:00,527
When you look at the roots,

580
00:24:00,528 --> 00:24:04,737
some of them are pink and thick.

581
00:24:04,738 --> 00:24:06,737
Those are the tasty ones.

582
00:24:06,738 --> 00:24:09,638
I recommend blanching them with the roots.

583
00:24:10,338 --> 00:24:12,847
What's the difference between island spinach and regular spinach?

584
00:24:12,848 --> 00:24:15,547
Regular spinach has straight stems.

585
00:24:15,548 --> 00:24:19,187
So it's just fragrant.

586
00:24:19,188 --> 00:24:21,117
But island spinach was grown...

587
00:24:21,118 --> 00:24:22,387
- with a lot of sea breezes. - It's just like the name.

588
00:24:22,388 --> 00:24:23,817
- So it's called island spinach. - Right.

589
00:24:23,818 --> 00:24:26,087
- Really? - Island spinach grows closely...

590
00:24:26,088 --> 00:24:27,627
to the ground and spreads out.

591
00:24:27,628 --> 00:24:29,897
So the leaves are thicker and sweeter.

592
00:24:29,898 --> 00:24:31,058
That's how they're different.

593
00:24:31,498 --> 00:24:32,968
I want siler divaricata...

594
00:24:33,498 --> 00:24:35,238
and coastal siler.

595
00:24:35,438 --> 00:24:37,808
- I want 200g each. - You want 200g each.

596
00:24:39,738 --> 00:24:41,178
Young sprouts of siler divaricata...

597
00:24:41,538 --> 00:24:44,038
are in season now.

598
00:24:44,108 --> 00:24:47,308
They're not chewy or bitter.

599
00:24:47,478 --> 00:24:50,278
If you pickle them right now,

600
00:24:50,418 --> 00:24:53,848
you can enjoy soft siler divaricata for a long time.

601
00:24:54,258 --> 00:24:55,317
So I love it.

602
00:24:55,318 --> 00:24:57,158
- I'd love to try some. - Same here.

603
00:24:57,788 --> 00:24:59,557
Your explanations about greens are so interesting.

604
00:24:59,558 --> 00:25:00,998
- Really? - She's the Greens Master.

605
00:25:01,958 --> 00:25:04,297
What Chef Anh Sung Jae said about you went viral.

606
00:25:04,298 --> 00:25:07,297
"I love that there's a young chef who loves greens."

607
00:25:07,298 --> 00:25:08,667
- Right. - Same here. I was grateful...

608
00:25:08,668 --> 00:25:09,767
- that you were on the show. - It's rare.

609
00:25:09,768 --> 00:25:11,237
- Young people don't like greens. - You like them as you get older.

610
00:25:11,238 --> 00:25:12,737
I really wanted...

611
00:25:12,738 --> 00:25:15,178
- Right? I bet. - to hear that from him.

612
00:25:16,278 --> 00:25:17,777
You don't have mugwort yet, right?

613
00:25:17,778 --> 00:25:20,307
No. Mugwort is taking a while to be prepared.

614
00:25:20,308 --> 00:25:21,377
It's been snowing.

615
00:25:21,378 --> 00:25:23,147
- It snowed on the field. Yes. - Because it's cold?

616
00:25:23,148 --> 00:25:25,164
I need to wait a little longer to buy henbit, right?

617
00:25:25,188 --> 00:25:28,017
Right. It's not available now.

618
00:25:28,018 --> 00:25:30,557
When I was young, I loved henbit flowers.

619
00:25:30,558 --> 00:25:31,888
Those flowers are also edible.

620
00:25:33,488 --> 00:25:34,898
Fatsia shoot looks great.

621
00:25:36,428 --> 00:25:37,527
I bet she wants to buy everything.

622
00:25:37,528 --> 00:25:39,488
It's impossible to stay calm at vegetable stalls.

623
00:25:40,128 --> 00:25:41,968
I'll take one.

624
00:25:43,138 --> 00:25:45,007
I wasn't going to buy fatsia shoot.

625
00:25:45,008 --> 00:25:46,408
It looks so fresh.

626
00:25:47,008 --> 00:25:49,278
- This one. - Gosh, it looks great.

627
00:25:49,778 --> 00:25:50,908
This is crazy.

628
00:25:51,078 --> 00:25:52,847
Silver wild chives don't have long leaves.

629
00:25:52,848 --> 00:25:54,477
- Right. - How do you usually eat them?

630
00:25:54,478 --> 00:25:55,947
They're good when they're stir-fried.

631
00:25:55,948 --> 00:25:58,717
You can eat them like you eat small onions.

632
00:25:58,718 --> 00:25:59,847
They're quite tasty.

633
00:25:59,848 --> 00:26:02,057
- I'll take canola greens. - Canola greens.

634
00:26:02,058 --> 00:26:03,688
I'll take 300g of it.

635
00:26:04,288 --> 00:26:06,587
- I'll take 200g of garlic chives. - Just 200g?

636
00:26:06,588 --> 00:26:08,227
I'll also take 100g of brachycarpa.

637
00:26:08,228 --> 00:26:10,509
- You also want brachycarpa. - They're all in season now.

638
00:26:10,968 --> 00:26:12,898
(Hold on a second.)

639
00:26:12,968 --> 00:26:14,997
How do you usually eat barley grass?

640
00:26:14,998 --> 00:26:16,238
Barley grass...

641
00:26:16,798 --> 00:26:18,467
- Are they barley sprouts? - Yes.

642
00:26:18,468 --> 00:26:19,768
(Barley grass)

643
00:26:20,168 --> 00:26:22,377
Many people add it to the fermented skate liver soup.

644
00:26:22,378 --> 00:26:24,077
People in the lower regions do that. People also add it...

645
00:26:24,078 --> 00:26:25,854
- to the kimchi or doenjang soup. - Fermented skate.

646
00:26:25,878 --> 00:26:29,418
If it's tender, you can season it and eat it.

647
00:26:29,948 --> 00:26:31,747
- Please give me 200g of it... - Okay.

648
00:26:31,748 --> 00:26:33,787
I can season and eat.

649
00:26:33,788 --> 00:26:36,057
You plan what to do as you buy the ingredients.

650
00:26:36,058 --> 00:26:37,087
That's right.

651
00:26:37,088 --> 00:26:38,657
How cool. It's my first time cooking barley grass.

652
00:26:38,658 --> 00:26:39,658
(Excited)

653
00:26:40,858 --> 00:26:41,997
Although you cook often,

654
00:26:41,998 --> 00:26:43,597
you can still find new edible greens.

655
00:26:43,598 --> 00:26:45,228
I know. I find it fascinating too.

656
00:26:46,268 --> 00:26:48,467
- That's all. - Is that all?

657
00:26:48,468 --> 00:26:50,708
(Please calculate the total including the barley grass.)

658
00:26:50,968 --> 00:26:52,267
- It's 71 dollars. - Did she say 71 dollars?

659
00:26:52,268 --> 00:26:53,628
About 70 dollars' worth of greens?

660
00:26:53,668 --> 00:26:56,388
- About 70 dollars' worth of greens? - I have just the right amount.

661
00:26:57,238 --> 00:26:58,247
(She pays in cash.)

662
00:26:58,248 --> 00:27:00,278
- Thank you. - Thank you.

663
00:27:02,418 --> 00:27:03,547
(Her bag gets filled with greens.)

664
00:27:03,548 --> 00:27:04,748
- Here you go. - Thank you.

665
00:27:05,718 --> 00:27:07,487
- Thank you. - Make tasty dishes.

666
00:27:07,488 --> 00:27:09,218
- Have a nice day. - Okay. Thank you.

667
00:27:11,358 --> 00:27:12,687
It must feel very nice.

668
00:27:12,688 --> 00:27:14,208
- It makes me the happiest. - Exactly.

669
00:27:15,458 --> 00:27:17,627
Chamsongi mushrooms, please.

670
00:27:17,628 --> 00:27:18,828
How about five dollars' worth?

671
00:27:21,238 --> 00:27:22,668
You must look so cute to her.

672
00:27:23,138 --> 00:27:25,238
- Please give me the hard ones. - Okay.

673
00:27:26,108 --> 00:27:27,368
- Hello. - Hello.

674
00:27:27,438 --> 00:27:28,607
Please give me two dollars' worth...

675
00:27:28,608 --> 00:27:30,137
- of green garlic. - Okay.

676
00:27:30,138 --> 00:27:31,977
- Thank you. - Okay. Bye.

677
00:27:31,978 --> 00:27:33,348
- Have a nice day. - Okay.

678
00:27:33,878 --> 00:27:35,317
The greens will become popular.

679
00:27:35,318 --> 00:27:36,977
- Seriously. They'll become popular. - Seriously.

680
00:27:36,978 --> 00:27:38,047
They'll become popular.

681
00:27:38,048 --> 00:27:39,894
(She goes outside the market after purchasing many items.)

682
00:27:39,918 --> 00:27:41,317
What's your favorite kind of greens?

683
00:27:41,318 --> 00:27:43,017
- My favorite? - Let's say you have to pick one.

684
00:27:43,018 --> 00:27:45,027
- It's a difficult question. - I'm curious.

685
00:27:45,028 --> 00:27:46,427
It'll be hard since she likes so many kinds.

686
00:27:46,428 --> 00:27:48,257
You can blanch them and season them,

687
00:27:48,258 --> 00:27:51,067
make fresh kimchi, or add them in soup.

688
00:27:51,068 --> 00:27:52,597
I like different kinds for different cooking styles.

689
00:27:52,598 --> 00:27:54,697
- Of course. - But if I must choose one...

690
00:27:54,698 --> 00:27:56,738
(Which kind of greens is Greens Master's favorite?)

691
00:27:57,668 --> 00:27:59,067
Daylily leaves.

692
00:27:59,068 --> 00:28:00,437
- Daylily leaves? - I love daylily leaves.

693
00:28:00,438 --> 00:28:01,478
What are those?

694
00:28:01,608 --> 00:28:03,337
- Daylily leaves. - I know what they are.

695
00:28:03,338 --> 00:28:05,177
- They look like spinach. - They're flat.

696
00:28:05,178 --> 00:28:07,747
That's right. Once they're blanched, they're very tender...

697
00:28:07,748 --> 00:28:09,118
and sweet.

698
00:28:09,278 --> 00:28:10,378
They're very tasty.

699
00:28:13,618 --> 00:28:16,957
(She comes back home with her hands heavy.)

700
00:28:16,958 --> 00:28:18,128
Her hands are heavy.

701
00:28:20,228 --> 00:28:22,198
(Rummaging)

702
00:28:23,398 --> 00:28:25,428
(She puts the greens on the kitchen counter.)

703
00:28:26,398 --> 00:28:27,838
You tied your hair.

704
00:28:27,938 --> 00:28:30,138
- It means that you'll cook. - It means that I'll cook.

705
00:28:31,768 --> 00:28:36,308
(She puts on an apron, her battle clothing.)

706
00:28:37,238 --> 00:28:39,447
(She picks up a bag of greens.)

707
00:28:39,448 --> 00:28:41,078
(Sniffing)

708
00:28:41,818 --> 00:28:43,318
It was my first time cooking it.

709
00:28:43,678 --> 00:28:44,888
- Barley grass. - Yes.

710
00:28:46,148 --> 00:28:48,388
(She rinses it thoroughly.)

711
00:28:49,358 --> 00:28:51,228
(She adds bay salt to the boiling water.)

712
00:28:51,758 --> 00:28:53,287
- She began to experiment. - Yes.

713
00:28:53,288 --> 00:28:55,798
I decided to test different methods.

714
00:28:56,498 --> 00:28:58,598
(How will I cook it in a fun way?)

715
00:28:59,828 --> 00:29:02,438
(She blanches the barley grass first.)

716
00:29:05,308 --> 00:29:06,968
You try it as it is.

717
00:29:10,708 --> 00:29:12,324
- Does it taste bad? - What's that facial expression?

718
00:29:12,348 --> 00:29:14,207
- She doesn't like it. - She doesn't like it.

719
00:29:14,208 --> 00:29:15,618
Let me boil it a bit more.

720
00:29:16,778 --> 00:29:18,247
(She blanches the barley grass a bit more in the hot saltwater.)

721
00:29:18,248 --> 00:29:20,048
(Slurping)

722
00:29:20,888 --> 00:29:23,918
(Perhaps it'll taste good if I rinse it with cold water.)

723
00:29:24,658 --> 00:29:26,387
None of the methods worked.

724
00:29:26,388 --> 00:29:27,457
(I see.)

725
00:29:27,458 --> 00:29:28,728
You tried different methods.

726
00:29:28,898 --> 00:29:30,998
This test was a failure.

727
00:29:31,668 --> 00:29:33,198
It came out too tough.

728
00:29:33,298 --> 00:29:35,098
How did it become tough when you blanched it?

729
00:29:35,138 --> 00:29:36,897
Isn't the taste similar...

730
00:29:36,898 --> 00:29:38,884
- no matter the texture? - It's totally different.

731
00:29:38,908 --> 00:29:40,967
- I see. - How does the barley grass taste?

732
00:29:40,968 --> 00:29:42,308
Is it sweet?

733
00:29:42,378 --> 00:29:44,477
It tastes like chive without the bite.

734
00:29:44,478 --> 00:29:45,607
It tastes like savory chive.

735
00:29:45,608 --> 00:29:46,748
(Fascinated)

736
00:29:46,778 --> 00:29:49,577
I thought about how to improve the tough texture.

737
00:29:49,578 --> 00:29:51,187
- Texture? - I'll stir-fry it...

738
00:29:51,188 --> 00:29:53,748
and fry it in the future.

739
00:29:54,688 --> 00:29:56,117
(Barley grass test: The texture is tough.)

740
00:29:56,118 --> 00:29:57,838
(She makes notes as soon as the test ends.)

741
00:29:59,058 --> 00:30:00,328
You make notes.

742
00:30:00,588 --> 00:30:02,058
- That way... - It's your fortune.

743
00:30:03,028 --> 00:30:06,528
It'll be a shame to use other people's recipes only.

744
00:30:07,128 --> 00:30:09,897
So I try different cooking methods...

745
00:30:09,898 --> 00:30:11,507
to find the right one.

746
00:30:11,508 --> 00:30:12,967
You write down all sorts of things.

747
00:30:12,968 --> 00:30:15,077
Do you study different cooking methods...

748
00:30:15,078 --> 00:30:16,107
- on a regular basis? - That's right.

749
00:30:16,108 --> 00:30:17,337
- That's impressive. - You continue to do that.

750
00:30:17,338 --> 00:30:18,677
- I got chills. - Really?

751
00:30:18,678 --> 00:30:20,147
(I got chills.)

752
00:30:20,148 --> 00:30:22,277
I keep simple ham recipes...

753
00:30:22,278 --> 00:30:23,777
on my phone.

754
00:30:23,778 --> 00:30:25,048
- Really? - I'm serious.

755
00:30:25,548 --> 00:30:27,148
(Chills)

756
00:30:27,618 --> 00:30:29,617
- It's an act of creation. - Exactly.

757
00:30:29,618 --> 00:30:30,618
You're right.

758
00:30:30,619 --> 00:30:33,099
(After the creation exercise, she goes back into the kitchen.)

759
00:30:34,328 --> 00:30:35,768
(She pours something into the bowl.)

760
00:30:35,828 --> 00:30:37,797
Short-necked clams. They have a refreshing taste.

761
00:30:37,798 --> 00:30:39,278
(She prepares the short-necked clam.)

762
00:30:40,698 --> 00:30:43,868
(She adds enough water and puts the pot over the fire.)

763
00:30:44,868 --> 00:30:47,808
(She adds a bit of rice wine to eliminate the fishy smell.)

764
00:30:49,638 --> 00:30:52,278
(I'll boil them like this.)

765
00:30:55,418 --> 00:30:57,477
(Let's see.)

766
00:30:57,478 --> 00:30:59,648
Green garlic. Perilla soft tofu.

767
00:31:00,918 --> 00:31:02,158
Island spinach.

768
00:31:02,988 --> 00:31:04,357
(Housewarming party)

769
00:31:04,358 --> 00:31:07,028
Today, I'll invite friends over for a housewarming party.

770
00:31:07,558 --> 00:31:10,328
I'll make dishes for the housewarming party.

771
00:31:10,428 --> 00:31:11,968
Today's dinner consists...

772
00:31:11,998 --> 00:31:14,637
of the pot rice using short-necked clams,

773
00:31:14,638 --> 00:31:17,598
perilla soft tofu jjigae with blue crabs,

774
00:31:17,808 --> 00:31:19,267
- and four kinds of greens. - That sounds delicious.

775
00:31:19,268 --> 00:31:20,367
- Blue crabs and perilla. - Fresh spring cabbage...

776
00:31:20,368 --> 00:31:21,508
and brachycarpa kimchi,

777
